  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) and its overtime provisions for executive, administrative, and professional employees, commonly referred to as the \"white collar\" exemptions. The FLSA, enacted in 1938, sets minimum wage, overtime pay, and recordkeeping requirements for most employees. To qualify for the white collar exemptions, employees must meet specific duties tests and earn a minimum salary.\n\nThe report discusses the history of the FLSA and the evolution of the white collar exemptions, including the various regulations issued by the Department of Labor (DOL). The current regulations require employees to earn a minimum salary of $684 per week ($35,568 annually) to qualify for the exemptions.\n\nThe report also covers the 2019 proposed rule, which would increase the minimum salary requirement to $35,308 annually ($679 per week) and allow for automatic adjustments to the salary threshold every three years. The proposed rule would also allow for up to 10% of the salary requirement to be satisfied through non-discretionary bonuses and incentive payments.\n\nThe report concludes by discussing the potential impact of the proposed rule on employers and employees, with estimates suggesting that approximately 1.3 million workers could become eligible for overtime pay if the rule is finalized. The report also notes that the 2016 final rule, which would have raised the salary threshold to $47,476 annually, was invalidated by a federal court and is currently on hold.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA) and its overtime provisions for executive, administrative, and professional employees, commonly referred to as the \"white collar\" exemptions. The FLSA, enacted in 1938, sets minimum wage, overtime pay, and recordkeeping requirements for most employees. To qualify for the white collar exemptions, employees must meet specific duties tests and earn a minimum salary.\n\nThe report discusses the history of the FLSA and the evolution of the white collar exemptions, including the various regulations issued by the Department of Labor (DOL). The current regulations require employees to earn a minimum salary of $684 per week ($35,568 annually) to qualify for the exemptions.\n\nThe report also covers the 2019 proposed rule, which would increase the minimum salary requirement to $35,308 annually ($679 per week) and allow for automatic adjustments to the salary threshold every three years. The proposed rule would also allow for up to 10% of the salary requirement to be satisfied through non-discretionary bonuses and incentive payments.\n\nThe report concludes by discussing the potential impact of the proposed rule on employers and employees, with estimates suggesting that approximately 1.3 million workers could become eligible for overtime pay if the rule is finalized. The report also notes that the 2016 final rule, which would have raised the salary threshold to $47,476 annually, was invalidated by a federal court and is currently on hold.",
